To answer your question, using == operator for string comparison is not recommended since it only compares the memory addresses occupied by two objects. This means that if you create two identical String objects with different variable names, they are considered as unequal since their memory addresses would differ. The equals() method on the other hand, is designed specifically for comparing content of two objects. Therefore, always use equals() method to compare two Strings as shown below:

```java
public class StringComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str1 = "Hey";
        String str2 = "Hey";

        if (str1 == str2) { // this will never work with strings!
            System.out.println("Both Strings are same");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Both Strings are different!");
        }

        if (str1.equals(str2)) { // this works perfectly fine
            System.out.println("Both Strings are same");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Both Strings are different!");
        }
        String anotherString = new String("Hello World");
        if (str1 == anotherString) {
            System.out.println("Both Strings are same");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Both Strings are different!");
        }

        if (str1.equals(anotherString)) {
            System.out.println("Both Strings are same");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Both Strings are different!");
        }
    }
}
```
In conclusion, using == operator for string comparison can lead to bugs or unexpected behavior, which can easily be avoided by using the equals() method.

